<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elazığ</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elaziz, Mamuret-ul-Aziz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elazığ is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on a plain east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. It was founded in the 19th century below the old fortress town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> and named Mamuret-ul-Aziz after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdulaziz. In ancient times its plain was the heart of the kingdom of Isuwa and later of Sophene, and many sites were excavated before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keban-dam">Keban Dam</a> flooded part of it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elazig.jpg" alt="Elazığ" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taha Koçak · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elaz%C4%B1%C4%9F_City_Center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Elazığ stands at the north-western corner of a valley about thirty miles long, known locally as Uluova, the Great Valley, and called Vosgetashd, the Golden Plain, by the area's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. The plain lies at an altitude of 1,067 metres in the uppermost valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Lake Hazar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> of several dams surround the city so closely that it resembles an inland peninsula. About five kilometres to the north-east, on a hill, lies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a>, a much older fortress town, of which Elazığ began as a suburb called Mezre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, who settled there about 2000 BCE. Harput and its region formed part of the kingdom of Urartu at its maximum extent, and may stand near Carcathiocerta, the first capital of the Kingdom of Sophene. Early Muslim geographers called it Ḥiṣn Ziyād. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 the region came under Turkish control, and it later passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>Mezre was a small hamlet, the residence of the Çötelizades, a notable family exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> in the late eighteenth century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resid-mehmed-pasha">Reşid Mehmed Pasha</a> made it a garrison, it grew into a small town with a prosperous Armenian middle class, and in 1869 Krikor Ipekjian founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> factory there. In 1878 it became the centre of the Mamuret-ul-Aziz Vilayet. The name shortened to Elaziz, and in 1937 an order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> turned it into Elazık, finally accepted as Elazığ.</p><p>In November 1895 government-backed Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> massacred and plundered the Armenian villages of the plain, and many residents were killed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. Kurdish rebels took the town in the Sheikh Said rebellion of 1925. In the Republican period Elazığ grew into a modern city while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> lay largely in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Elazığ is the capital of Elazığ Province and had 387,072 inhabitants in 2022, a mixed population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and Turks. Fırat University was founded there in 1975. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keban-dam">Keban Dam</a>, built between 1966 and 1974, flooded formerly populated areas and affected more than 30,000 people, and many of those displaced settled in the city. Few historic monuments stand in the city itself, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harput">Harput</a> keeps its castle and the Ulu Camii, built in 1156 by the Artuqid sultan Fahrettin Karaaslan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elazığ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isuwa lay in the <b>Elazığ</b> plain, east of the Euphrates in eastern Anatolia, a region rich in copper.</blockquote><a href="/state/isuwa">Isuwa</a></li><li><blockquote>Sophene lay west of the Tigris sources around modern <b>Elazığ</b>, the old land of Isuwa.</blockquote><a href="/state/sophene">Sophene</a></li></ul></section>
